<p>
  Complete the sentences below to explain why <m>r(x)</m> behaves the way it does near <m>x = 4</m>, near <m>x=1</m>, and near <m>x=-1</m>.
</p>
<div sectionWideCheckWork colorAnswersSeparately>
<p>
  As <m>x</m> gets closer and closer to <m>4</m>, <answer><mathInput minWidth="25"><label>the numerator <m>p(x)=x^2-1</m> is approaching</label></mathInput>15</answer>, while <answer><mathInput minWidth="20"><label>the denominator <m>q(x)=x^2-3x-4</m> approaches</label></mathInput>0</answer>. <answer><choiceInput inline><label>This means that <m>r(x)=\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}</m></label><choice>approaches <m>0</m></choice><choice credit="1">increases or decreases without bound</choice><choice>approaches <m>0.4</m></choice><choice>approaches two different numbers</choice></choiceInput></answer> near <m>x = 4</m>.  <answer><choiceInput inline><label>The function <m>r(x)</m> has a </label><choice>zero</choice>
  <choice>hole</choice><choice>jump</choice><choice credit="1">vertical asymptote</choice></choiceInput></answer> at <m>x=4</m>.
</p>
</div>

<div sectionWideCheckWork colorAnswersSeparately>
<p>
  As <m>x</m> gets closer and closer to <m>1</m>, <answer><mathInput minWidth="20"><label>the numerator <m>p(x)=x^2-1</m> is approaching</label></mathInput>0</answer>, while <answer><mathInput minWidth="25"><label>the denominator <m>q(x)=x^2-3x-4</m> approaches</label></mathInput>-6</answer>. <answer><choiceInput inline><label>This means that <m>r(x)=\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}</m></label><choice credit="1">approaches <m>0</m></choice><choice>increases or decreases without bound</choice><choice>approaches <m>0.4</m></choice><choice>approaches two different numbers</choice></choiceInput></answer> near <m>x = 1</m>.  <answer><choiceInput inline><label>The function <m>r(x)</m> has a </label><choice credit="1">zero</choice>
  <choice>hole</choice><choice>jump</choice><choice>vertical asymptote</choice></choiceInput></answer> at <m>x=1</m>.
</p>
</div>

<div sectionWideCheckWork colorAnswersSeparately>
<p>
  As <m>x</m> gets closer and closer to <m>-1</m>, <answer><mathInput minWidth="20"><label>the numerator <m>p(x)=x^2-1</m> is approaching</label></mathInput>0</answer>, and <answer><mathInput minWidth="20"><label>the denominator <m>q(x)=x^2-3x-4</m> also approaches</label></mathInput>0</answer>. Factoring both the numerator and denominator results in <m>\frac{(x-1)(x+1)}{(x-4)(x+1)}</m>. As long as <m>x\neq -1</m>, the two <m>(x+1)</m> terms cancel, resulting in <m>\frac{x-1}{x-4}</m>. <answer><choiceInput inline><label>This means that <m>r(x)=\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}</m></label><choice>approaches <m>0</m></choice><choice>increases or decreases without bound</choice><choice credit="1">approaches <m>\frac{-2}{-5}=0.4</m></choice><choice>approaches two different numbers</choice></choiceInput></answer> near <m>x = -1</m>, even though <m>r(x)</m> is undefined at <m>x=-1</m>.  <answer><choiceInput inline><label>The function <m>r(x)</m> has a </label><choice>zero</choice>
  <choice credit="1">hole</choice><choice>jump</choice><choice>vertical asymptote</choice></choiceInput></answer> at <m>x=-1</m>.
</p>
</div>

<p>
  Finally, the graph of <m>r(x)</m> appears below.
</p>

<graph>
  <shortDescription>graph of the function r of x</shortDescription>
  $r
  <legend position="upperLeft">
    <label><m>\displaystyle r(x)=\frac{x^2-1}{x^2-3x-4}</m></label>
  </legend>
</graph>

<p>
  Can you see all the behavior we just discussed, or only some of it? Remember that you can zoom in and out using the "+" and "-" in the lower right corner of the graph, and that the "O" returns to the original view.
  <answer><choiceInput selectMultiple><label>Select the features of <m>r(x)</m> which appear on the graph.</label><choice credit="1">zero</choice><choice>hole</choice><choice>jump</choice><choice credit="1">vertical asymptote</choice></choiceInput></answer>
</p>